{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head 154d60127f7efd84170999a6e5ad001191faf624 on request/performance-instrumentation, naming primary artifact b62527e2524872670ca60ffcbd24c1d322be08c7 at internal/observe and resting on all eleven code artifacts at that commit.\n\nWHAT I RAN, AT THAT EXACT COMMIT, IN A DETACHED WORKTREE. go build ./... clean. go vet ./... clean. go test -race on internal/observe, internal/telemetry, internal/app, internal/service, internal/kernel: all pass. make vet: clean. make test: FAILS.\n\nBLOCKING 1 — THE HEAD FAILS ITS OWN GATE. make test is red at this commit:\n\n  --- FAIL: TestGateSurfaceCoversEveryCLISubcommand\n      docs/reference/gs/serve.md: documented flags do not match the implementation:\n        undocumented: --otel-endpoint, --profile-listen\n\nI checked whether this is pre-existing rather than yours. It is not: the same test at main c19d8a01 passes. The two new gs serve flags introduce it. The request frames this as the code head before documentation integration, and I take that intent, but the flag surface gate is inside make test, and the conditions of this review name the full Go gates. docs/reference/gs/serve.md is a small edit and belongs in this head.\n\nBLOCKING 2 — THE FINITE-LABEL BOUND IS NOT HELD BY ANY TEST. routeTemplate's length ceiling and character allowlist (internal/observe/http.go:65-81) are the only thing keeping http.route bounded if Pattern ever carries non-static content — the comment says so, that it \"fails closed if a future dynamic router violates that rule\". Nothing holds it. I deleted the guard, returning pattern unconditionally, and both internal/observe and internal/telemetry stayed green. No test in the repository references routeTemplate, the \"other\" fallback, or the \"unmatched\" fallback. Route templates and finite labels are named conditions of this review; an unheld guard is one a later refactor removes in silence. Please cover the over-length pattern, the disallowed-character pattern, and the empty pattern.\n\nBLOCKING 3 — THE PRIVACY ASSERTION IN THE TELEMETRY TEST CANNOT FAIL. TestInMemoryExporterGetsTemplateOnlyHTTPMetrics scans span.Attributes for a leaked raw path, but observer.Record attaches the operation attributes with AddEvent, so they land in span.Events[].Attributes, which the loop never reads. Proof by mutation: I changed HTTPHandler to pass Route: request.URL.Path, so the raw path \"/v0/actors/private-fingerprint\" flowed straight into those event attributes. internal/telemetry still passed. The behaviour itself is correct and is genuinely held elsewhere — observe.TestHTTPHandlerUsesRouteTemplateNotRawPath failed on that same mutation, which is how I know the seam works. But the test whose name and failure message claim to protect privacy at the exporter is vacuous, and it never asserts the metric attributes, which is where http.route actually ships. Please assert over span events and over the collected metric attributes.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers touched, per docs/reference/architecture.md: the kernel, the application layer (internal/app, internal/service), storage (internal/gitstore), plus two new packages. The contract is preserved. internal/observe is a leaf: it imports nothing of ours, carries no application vocabulary into the kernel, and the kernel uses only OperationVerify and cache-shape paths, reached through the context rather than through a constructor. internal/telemetry is the only package that names OpenTelemetry, and it is imported solely by cmd/gs and the perf scenario runner. That is the composition-boundary rule the request asked for, and it holds. I do not think this head changes the layer contract, so I am not asking for an architecture.md edit here; when the documentation head lands, that page should name the observation seam, because a cross-cutting package the kernel imports is exactly the kind of fact the canonical layer account should carry.\n\nSECURITY. No defect I would block on, three things to record. Exporter configuration is explicit and never discovered: NewOTLP with an empty endpoint returns a disabled Runtime, and default gs serve enables nothing, which I confirmed by mutation — making Begin return a live closure when the observer is nil fails TestDisabledBeginDoesNothing. The profiler is opt-in, bound through validateLoopbackListen, and closed on context cancellation. First: the profile mux registers GET /debug/pprof/cmdline, which returns the process argv, and gs serve's argv contains the absolute repository path. That is the one profiling route that exposes a filesystem path, and it is not needed to take a profile. I would drop it. Second: the new listener has no Host-header allowlist, so it inherits the DNS-rebinding gap that open request 186a98cf already names for the main resident. Not a regression, and out of scope here, but it is a second loopback listener now standing on the same unfixed ground. Third: the module graph goes from two requirements to twenty-six, and because cmd/gs imports internal/telemetry unconditionally, gRPC, protobuf, genproto and grpc-gateway are linked into every gs binary even though only the OTLP/HTTP exporters are used. Activation is opt-in; linkage is not. That is inherent to OTLP and worth stating rather than fixing.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. One concrete opportunity that does not weaken any condition. Begin returns a nil func when observation is off, so every call site carries an if done != nil, and in AcceptSubmission that forced a resultErr variable threaded through four return paths purely to feed the deferred call. A no-op closure would delete that dance at two call sites at the cost of one allocation on a path that already runs git. If the allocation matters, keep the nil and say so where Begin is defined, because the current comment explains the allocation but not the burden it moves onto callers. Smaller: in snapshotWithSource, foldStarted is declared at app.go:1059 and shadowed again at 1070, so the outer binding is live only in the Full branch; declaring it inside each branch would read straighter. I checked ResponseObserver before suggesting its removal and it is genuinely used at server.go:623, so it stays.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED AND FOUND CORRECT. Default no-op behaviour, by mutation. Raw request paths never reaching the observer, by mutation. The bounded vocabulary: Operation, Path and Outcome are closed constant sets, Classify collapses arbitrary errors to five outcomes, GitPath collapses Git's command surface to five cost classes, method collapses to three verbs plus OTHER, and status is recorded as a class rather than a code. Go runtime metrics are started against the same meter provider and asserted present. The overhead mode is sound: it refuses unless base and candidate resolve to the same commit, refuses a dirty harness checkout, and points both revisions at the running executable so the only difference between the paired samples is the --telemetry flag. I traced binaries[CandidateRevision] to its seed at main.go:242 before concluding that, because the overhead branch assigns only BaseRevision and I wanted to be sure the candidate was not empty. Everything above the three blocking items is ready.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b62527e2524872670ca60ffcbd24c1d322be08c7","head":"154d60127f7efd84170999a6e5ad001191faf624","verdict":"changes-requested"}}